{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794527372697,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","part":{"id":"prt_1d22aa59900121SFiC3gwOVeH6","sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","messageID":"msg_1d22aa0bc001jok7jvmhwR4Jfz","type":"step-start","snapshot":"7b7499e8ca6fec18aae1cf1d66516a05a9730232"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527373810,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22aa8e50016eqKntT1Olkqgo","sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","messageID":"msg_1d22aa0bc001jok7jvmhwR4Jfz","type":"tool","id":"call_5c0dd45c60bb402cbc7bea51","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527373541,"end":1794527373810}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527373814,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22aa7b3002B12UbLSM3qLi1e","sessionID":"ses_e2dd8cae6ffeV3iKQc8fxV7h6K","messageID":"msg_1d22aa0bc001jok7jvmhwR4Jfz","type":"tool","id":"call_b2046739bd5a4aa29aff10ed","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527373235,"end":1794527373814}}}}
